## Rust on the full corpus: the sweep and the gates

Corpus cut at U64 by the port's own bundler: `~/units-82cb77a56976`, 1,433
units (U62: 1,353); `~/units-current` points at it. Five tests refuse to
resolve by design (they cite the manifest-only `Codex`/`Emit` quires or a
missing chapter); `ops/cap-grant-emit` is the new one.

**Sweep** (every upstream test with an `.expected`, on codexrun):

    U62 corpus, this binary     856 of 1,081
    U64 corpus, before the fix  873 of 1,135   lost 4, gained 0, 54 new (21 ok)
    U64 corpus, after the fix   875 of 1,135   +tier1-latin-print, +tier2-print

The four LOST are engine and GOP render tests that now call `__heap-base`,
a U63 builtin with no interpreter rule: port work, not a meaning change.

**Two intended meaning changes in U63, caught and followed** (rust
`d39278d`): x86's CCE print tables had two
defects that the interpreter and the Roc text module emulated on purpose.
U63 fixed both, and new upstream tests pin them:

- `tier1-slice-bases` now starts slices 0 and 1 at U+0080 and U+0100 (was
  U+00C0 and U+0140, so every character in U+0080..U+017F printed 0x40 high:
  `ß` came out `ğ`). `ops/tier1-latin-print`.
- `__cce_print_multi` sign-extends the tier-2 delta (`movsxd`); it used to add
  it unsigned, so negative-delta slices printed an overlong sequence (`€`,
  `☀`, `…`, `•` as U+FFFD). `ops/tier2-print`.

This is the sweep doing the job Steve means it for: a change in what Codex
prints, found by a program that prints differently under Rust.

**Also real, not fixed:** `cce-foreign-byte`'s raw path. `char-encode` of
code -1 writes one unit (255) upstream and four in the interpreter. It is the
path the test itself calls "the defect the helper closes"; U63's new
`cce-foreign-byte-text` helper is interpreted correctly.

**Check gate: 1,394 agree, 38 differ** (U62: 1,352 / 0). Every difference
is a rule U63 or U64 added on purpose that Rust has not ported:

    17  we accept, Codex refuses   CDX1077 1078 1079 (narrower language), 2070
                                   (literal match needs a catch-all), 2085 x2
                                   2099 x3 2100 x2 (equality on Real/vector/type
                                   variable), 2093 x2 (lane past end), 2050 2051
                                   (bounded-unit narrowing), 2001 x2 (mask of the
                                   wrong lane type: our (vec-mask N) has no lane
                                   slot)
    15  both refuse, other code    8 quotes-* and lazy-reserved (ours CDX1000 or
                                   2001, U63's 1077 first); 1075 do-block; 1076
                                   ctor-field-comma; 4 generic-eq-* refusals
                                   (ours CDX3008)
    4   error counts differ        CDX1070 x2, 1071, 1024 (newline rules)
    1   we refuse, Codex accepts   generic-eq
    1   we crash, Codex refuses    generic-eq-unconstrained (ours `?`)

**Linear gate: 25 agree, 0 differ, GREEN.**

**Generic equality by dictionary passing (U64's feature) is not in Rust.**
`generic-eq` fails in the sweep (`if` on a function: `==` on a type variable
evaluates to no usable equality) and in the check gate. It is the largest
port item this Update opens.

## Rust made stingy: U63's refusals ported (rust `e83bd2a`)

Steve: "I want Rust to be stingy like Codex is now stingy." Every refusal U63
added is ported, and the check gate goes **1,394/38 -> 1,424 agree, 8
differ**; all 8 are generic equality (below). Linear gate green; sweep 875,
none lost; curated and safari unchanged on every Rust arm; cargo test 273.

Parser: CDX1078 (tokens left in a body), 1077 (a dropped token at columns
1-3; column 1 was CDX1000), 1075 (`do` body), 1076 (`(A, B)` field), 1079
(discarded literal statement), U63's CDX1070 continuation rule, and header
`trusting` lines. Checker: vector and withheld-record equality (2085/2099),
VectorMask carries its lane type (and the IR spells `(vector-mask n T)`),
Real width/mode mismatches are conflicts, lane range for the 256-bit
families (2093), literal-match coverage (2070), bounded-unit narrowing at
record sets and returns (2050/2051). Desugarer: `eq-withheld-set`.

The full gate caught two false refusals on the way, both fixed:
`raytracer-test` (Rust derived `__eq_RtObject` over a record holding a
Real, which U63 withholds transitively) and `char-open-at-eol` (a second
error on the lexer's own error token).

Process note: the check gate runs `~/build/rust-target/release/checkdump`
per unit, so rebuilding during a gate mixes builds. Gate a snapshot:
`CHECKDUMP=<copy> ./corpus_check_gate.sh`.

## Generic equality in Rust (rust `7b15035`): check gate 1,432 agree, 0 differ

`Eq a =>` is a derived class in the desugarer (dictionary `__d-Eq-a` when any
parameter mentions `a`; primitive `__eq_` wrappers; the call's dictionary
marked by an empty span), and the checker keeps upstream's eq-sites and judges
them per definition (CDX2100 on an unconstrained signature variable; 2085,
2099 at a call that binds a Real, a vector or a withheld type). The sweep gains
`generic-eq` (876 of 1,135, none lost): the interpreter prints all 30 rows.

**Every check-gate difference from U63 and U64 is closed.** Left for IR
parity only: lowering `==` through the dictionary, passing a caller's own
dictionary on, and the minted `__eqd_` helpers (the design's steps 3 and 4).
Until then `irdump` refuses `generic-eq` rather than emit wrong IR. No curated
or safari unit uses `Eq a =>`, so every Rust arm is unchanged.

## Rust IR parity, step 1: the equality helpers (rust `b593ffd`)

Rust named instantiated equality helpers but never minted them; upstream has
attached them on the IR path since U56. Now ported (`src/eq_helpers.rs`,
upstream's `eq-helper-defs`), with the list arm in lowering and U64's derived
record equality. A corpus-wide sweep of Rust IR against codexir (new tool,
scratch `irsweep.sh`: old irdump, new irdump, upstream, per unit; 16 minutes):

    1,036 SAME -> SAME    4 DIFF -> SAME    0 regressions
      131 DIFF -> DIFF  261 refused by Rust's IR path, before and after

The four: eq-generic-fields, eq-generic-recursive, list-equality,
record-equality. Check gate 1,432 / 0, sweep 876, curated and safari
unchanged. Step 2 (the Eq dictionary lowering and `__eqd_` helpers) next.
